<article class="env-lemma" id="00Z7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00Z7">Lemma <span data-tag="00Z7">7.47.9</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category. Let $\{ J_ i\} _{i\in I}$ be a set of topologies. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
The rule $J(U) = \bigcap J_ i(U)$ defines a topology on $\mathcal{C}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
There is a coarsest topology finer than all of the topologies $J_ i$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The first part is direct from the definitions. The second follows by taking the intersection of all topologies finer than all of the $J_ i$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>